(* ============================================================================
   PART 0: LAYOUT LAYER (L0) -- LINE AUTOMATON (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   The block productions (PARTS 1-2) are context-free only OVER THE OUTPUT OF
   THIS LAYER. Indentation, container prefixes, and lazy continuation are 2D
   properties no string grammar can express (they need column arithmetic and
   carried container state); L0 states them as a deterministic one-pass line
   automaton instead of prose.

   INPUT.  Lines, split on `newline` as that production defines it below.

           A LEADING BYTE ORDER MARK IS STRIPPED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-001]. A single U+FEFF
           at the very start of the document is removed before the first line
           is read, so `<BOM># T` is a heading and not paragraph text. ONE, and
           only there: a U+FEFF anywhere else is an ordinary zero-width
           character, and zero-width characters (U+200B, U+FEFF) are NOT
           whitespace and ARE ordinary destination characters.

           A NULL IS REPLACED BEFORE THE FIRST LINE IS READ -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-002].
           Every U+0000 in the source becomes U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER,
           wherever it stands, before the first line is read. It is therefore
           not content, and it is the ONE C0 control PART 9 §29 does not make
           content; CARVE-P9-061 carries that carve-out. EVERY occurrence is replaced,
           one U+0000 to one U+FFFD, so the transform does not change source
           offsets.

           ILL-FORMED INPUT IS REPLACED BEFORE THE FIRST LINE IS READ -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-023].
           A document is a sequence of Unicode scalar values. Every ill-formed
           sequence in the input becomes U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER before
           the first line is read, the same way U+0000 is replaced under
           CARVE-P0-002. Two shapes are ill-formed: an invalid UTF-8 byte
           sequence in a file or stream, which takes one U+FFFD per maximal
           subpart (the count the WHATWG UTF-8 decoder gives), and a lone
           surrogate in a string handed to an API, which takes one U+FFFD per
           unpaired code unit. Ill-formed input is replaced, never rejected,
           so every HTML and AST string a processor emits is well-formed. A
           paired surrogate is a well-formed astral character and is left
           alone, and a noncharacter (U+FFFE, U+FDD0) is a scalar value and
           therefore content. The rule covers the document handed to the
           processor, not an included file: an include whose bytes are not
           well-formed UTF-8 is refused as non-text under PART 6 I7, not
           replaced.

           Column arithmetic is PART 9 §24 C1. A TAB IS SYNTAX ONLY IN THE
           LEADING RUN (CARVE-P7-001).
   STATE.  open := stack of open containers, bottom-up (document ->
           innermost), plus the current leaf (none | paragraph | heading |
           ...). Container kinds and the per-line prefix each demands:
             block quote    marker `>` [end of line | one space]
             list item      indentation to content_column (PART 9 §24 C3)
             definition     indentation to the body column its separator
               body         sets (CARVE-P2-016)
             footnote def   indentation >= 2 spaces; the prefix CONSUMED is
                            exactly 2, content_column := 2 (PART 9 §16)
             opaque body    (code / raw / matched comment span) -- no
                            per-line prefix; verbatim up to its where-guarded
                            closer
             colon body     (admonition / div / figure group / fenced quote /
                            hard-break block / line block) -- no per-line
                            prefix; parsed as blocks (a line block as lines,
                            PART 9 §23) up to its exact-length closer or the
                            end of its host.

           A list item, a definition body and a footnote body are the
           INDENTATION CONTAINERS.

           EVERY FRAME CARRIES OWNERSHIP AND PARAGRAPH STATE SEPARATELY --
           NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-003]. A frame is the record

             owner              document | quote | list_item |
                                definition_body | footnote_body |
                                opaque_body | colon_body
             base_column        authored column at which this frame opened
             content_column     minimum column of ordinary child blocks
             container_open     whether the frame may own another line
             paragraph_open     whether its current leaf accepts lazy text
             last_boundary_kind ordinary | blank | line_comment |
                                fenced_comment | definition | attribute_line |
                                heading | thematic_break | quote | code_fence |
                                raw_fence | colon_fence | table | nested_list |
                                continuation | end

           `container_open` is inferred from `paragraph_open` only below the
           content column (O3). Elsewhere `paragraph_open` describes an
           operation available inside a frame that has already survived; it
           does not keep that frame alive.

           T  TRANSITIONS. Once a line or block has its owner, the boundary it
           forms sets both facts in every frame that contains it. `C` means
           the frame stays open after the boundary; `P` independently says
           whether a paragraph is open. `deepest P` means the last child
           container is followed to its deepest leaf. The table applies to
           list items, quotes, definition bodies and footnote bodies alike.

             preceding boundary       container_open   paragraph_open
             ordinary line             C                yes
             blank line                C                no
             line comment              C                no
             fenced comment            C                no
             invisible definition      C                no, for full extent
             attribute line            C                no
             heading                   C                no
             thematic break            C                no
             block quote               C                deepest P
             closed code/raw fence     C                no
             closed colon fence        C                no
             table                     C                no
             nested list               C                deepest P
             continuation marker       C                deepest attached P
             end of input              no               no

           A closed block therefore has ONE effect in every containing frame:
           it leaves the frame open and leaves no paragraph open. A following
           line may close the frame because its column is owned by an ancestor;
           the closed block itself never closes its owner.

   BLOCK STRUCTURE IS ONE ORDERED PROCEDURE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-024].
   For each normalized line, execute O1 to O4 below once, in order and without
   backtracking, with X1 to X3 as named exceptions. O1 matches prefixes and
   handles code and raw payload and comments before visible classification.
   Code and raw payload skip later steps: a closer applies T, while other
   payload lines leave all frame state unchanged. Comments use O2 and X1 to
   select or retain an owner, O4 to close frames deeper than that owner, and
   T for their state change. O2 selects a visible line's owner. Recognize the
   residue in that owner's coordinates and scope under PART 8 and its construct
   guards. Apply PART 9 §10 before O3 tests for a lazy fold. X1 may retain a
   deeper owner only after X1's recognition gate succeeds. Without a fold, O4
   closes frames deeper than the selected or retained owner and reads the line
   there. Apply T at each boundary, keeping container survival, paragraph
   openness and continuation claims separate. Only a construct's declared
   closer guard may read ahead.
   The continuation marker's declared closer guard reads ahead for the next
   block's column and kind, including the sibling and no-block checks.
   The clauses after X3 state consequences of these steps.

     O1 STRIP PREFIXES, OUTWARD-IN. Walk `open` bottom-up, consuming each
        container's prefix from L: strip `>` or `> ` for a quote, DEDENT by
        columns for an indentation container (PART 9 §24 C5). Stop at the
        first container whose prefix L does not supply. Each strip is measured
        in the coordinates the enclosing strip produced; host kind, prefix
        order and nesting depth do not alter that. Indentation never supplies
        a quote marker. A BLANK line supplies every indentation prefix and no
        quote marker: it closes the open paragraph and no indentation
        container (T), and without its `>` it ends a quote and the containers
        opened inside it (CARVE-P0-019).

        AN OPAQUE BODY TAKES THE LINE FIRST. If the innermost matched
        container is an opaque body, L is verbatim content unless it matches
        that fence's where-guarded closer (then pop). No other step applies to
        L. A colon body is not opaque: its lines go on to O2.

        COMMENTS ARE CLASSIFIED BEFORE BLOCK OWNERSHIP -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-005].
        Honor an active opaque payload first, and decode the container
        prefix and authored indentation once. Then recognize `%%` and a
        matched `%%%` span as layout-transparent comment tokens BEFORE O2
        selects an owner for visible block classification. Record the comment
        node, its authored spelling and its complete source extent, but remove
        it from visible classification and rendering. This is lexical erasure
        from layout, NEVER erasure from the AST or source map.

        A comment owned inside a surviving container applies the
        `line_comment` / `fenced_comment` row of T in both forms: the
        container remains open and the current paragraph closes. This
        transition does not put an enclosing-context comment fence inside an
        item it already ended. A following blank repeats the blank transition;
        it does not close the container merely because a comment preceded it.
        The next VISIBLE line takes O2 to O4. A comment stores no continuation
        claim (CARVE-P0-006); retention is X1, a separate rule and not parser
        fallthrough.

        A matched `%%%` token carries its opener, body and exact-width
        closer as one source extent across authored indentation. Its body is
        opaque. An opener without an exact-width closer is one `%%` line
        comment; later lines are classified normally. Comment delimiters
        inside code, raw content or another opaque payload are literal
        bytes. Nested containers, quotes, definition bodies and footnote
        bodies use this same ordering.

     O2 OWNER. The innermost frame whose prefix L supplied owns L. In an
        indentation frame a line AT the content column, or PAST it, supplied
        the prefix. A line BELOW the content column did not, at any column of
        that band: its owner is the nearest surviving ancestor whose content
        column it reaches, and it stays with the deeper frame only by O3 or
        X1. A quote is reached by its marker or by O3, never by a column.
        After a blank, a line below an item's content column is outside that
        item, the band above its base included. A sibling marker, and a list
        marker at an open item's content column (PART 9 §24 C3), are read as
        markers in O4 before O3 or X1 can keep the line.

     O3 FOLD. If a paragraph is open at the deepest frame, and the residue is
        not an interrupting line (PART 9 §10), L folds into that paragraph
        whatever prefixes it missed. The deepest frame is found by following
        the last child container to its leaf at every depth. Nothing closes.
        The fold changes the leaf operation, never which container survives.
        Recognition uses the owner O2 selected, its coordinates, and the
        construct's scope. PART 9 §10 applies the interruption policy to that
        recognition result; it does not recognize a different spelling for
        folding. ` # H` under `- a` is no heading at document column 1, so it
        folds, and `# H` at column 0 is one, so it does not. Reuse a result only
        within that owner and coordinate system. Fence guards still use the
        owning body's extent; comments take O1 before visible recognition.

        NO STORED CONTINUATION CLAIM, NO LAZY LINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-006].
        Apply T to the recognized boundary in its owner. An ordinary line
        leaves a paragraph open and stores a continuation claim for that
        frame. A boundary whose T row closes the paragraph stores no claim.
        A container row with `deepest P` or `deepest attached P` takes
        the state of its last-child leaf. An empty or closed container
        supplies no open leaf (CARVE-P0-017).
        Container survival, paragraph openness and stored ownership remain
        separate: a surviving frame does not by itself admit a lazy line.
        O3 needs both a claim for the leaf and an open paragraph there; it
        never searches earlier blocks or ancestor paragraphs for a claim.
        For `. >` / `X` the empty quote supplies neither, so `X` starts a
        document paragraph. `- > q` / `lazy` folds because `q` stored a claim
        and left the quoted paragraph open. Definition-term continuation
        remains governed by CARVE-P2-028.

        An ended descendant supplies no claim to an enclosing list item. If a
        later line reaches that item's content column and starts a paragraph,
        its ordinary-line transition stores a new claim. Thus
        `- - - ~~~` / `  tail` / `tail` puts both `tail` lines in the outer
        item: the first starts its paragraph and the second continues it.

        A QUOTE'S LAST BLOCK MAY ITSELF BE A QUOTE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-009].
        The last-child traversal in O3 applies at every depth, including a
        quote whose last block is another quote. CARVE-P0-006 and T decide
        whether its leaf can take a lazy line. If it cannot, a below-column
        line closes the missed frames and starts a block in O2's owner; no
        surviving frame adopts it merely because it once held a paragraph.
        X1 is the retained-ownership exception. In corpus
        75-list-nesting-and-looseness-4 the inner heading leaves no claim or
        open paragraph, so `lazy` starts a document paragraph.

        A wrapped attribute block applies T once across its complete source
        extent. In `- {.a` / `  .b}` / `tail`, the attribute_line transition
        leaves no claim or open paragraph, so `tail` is outside the item.
        A block attribute opened at a container's column is one construct, and
        its continuation lines may sit at any column: `- a` / `  {.c` / `}` /
        `para` reads like the one-line `{.c}`, so the attribute reaches no
        block and `para` is outside the item (markup-carve/carve#3421).
        Its continuation lines may sit at any column within the quote prefix
        of the container that opened it. A line that drops a `>` can only lazily
        continue an open paragraph and never closes the attribute.

        A LAZY FOLD KEEPS THE CONTAINER OPEN FOR FOLLOWING LINES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-015].
        A lazy fold closes no container. A later line returning to the
        container's content column remains inside it:

            - x
              :::
              a
            d
              b
              :::

        `a`, `d` and `b` form one paragraph in one div. With no open paragraph,
        the flush-left line ends the container and later lines are outside it.

     O4 OTHERWISE CLASSIFY IN THE OWNER. Close every frame deeper than the
        owner and read the residue there: BLANK -> close the open paragraph
        (recorded for tight/loose, PART 9 §17 L1); a BLOCK OPENER -> the
        interruption relation decides (PART 9 §10); a LIST MARKER reaching
        content_column -> sublist (PART 9 §24 C3); otherwise a new paragraph.
        An indentation frame reads a recognized opener at or past its content
        column, on its authored base (CARVE-P0-004). The document and a quote
        need the exact column for openers other than list markers
        (PART 9 §24 C4). When the residue is a container opener (a
        quote marker; a list marker at an admissible column; a `+`
        continuation marker at an open container's marker column or directly
        after a quote prefix, or first after the last opener on its line,
        PART 9 §17 L3/L4 and markup-carve/carve#3421; a
        fence opener whose closer-lookahead guard holds when it
        interrupts or would consume a host closer), push the container and
        recurse on the remaining residue.

        Ordinary prose reopens a paragraph anywhere among a container's
        blocks, including after a table, fence or quote, and following lazy
        lines then continue it (O3). A line at an item's content column remains
        inside the item; under a div it is the div's second block. A missing
        closer on a colon container alters none of this. A definition either
        registers or remains visible text; it MUST NOT disappear from both
        rendering and definition tables.

        ONE AUTHORED BLOCK BASE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-004]. Every open
        container which accepts child blocks has a minimum
        `content_column`. A recognized block opener AT that column opens
        there. In an INDENTATION CONTAINER (a list item, a definition body
        or a footnote body) a recognized opener PAST it establishes its
        authored visual column as the local `base_column` for that ONE
        complete block; the opener, payload, structural continuations,
        metadata and closer are measured from that base. A closer written
        below that base is measured as a payload line there is: once the
        line's available prefix is gone, a run matching the opener closes
        the block. Canonical output
        returns the block to the container's minimum column. Ordinary
        over-indented text establishes no base.

        THE DOCUMENT AND A BLOCK QUOTE DO NOT REBASE. There an opener is
        recognized at the content column exactly, except that list markers
        may open at any column (PART 9 §24 C4). An over-indented non-list opener
        is text: `   # H` is a paragraph at the document level and after
        `> `. The frame that answers is the owner O2 selected.

        EVERY RECOGNIZED BLOCK OPENER QUALIFIES, the line-block opener
        `::: |` included. A colon body has no prefix, so it answers as its
        host does: inside a colon body an indentation container hosts, an
        opener past the column opens on its authored base, and closes a
        paragraph of that body open above it, as in every indentation host
        (CARVE-P2-017). So under `- a` / `  ::: box`, a code fence run at
        column 4 opens a code block in the box.

        OWNERSHIP PRECEDES REBASING. While an authored block is open, a
        nested body's opener at its own content column is owned by that
        nested body; it MUST NOT be reinterpreted as a fresh sibling base in
        an ancestor. A blank line ends a paragraph, not the authored block
        which contains it. Blocks attached by a continuation marker apply this
        same rule; their construct clauses only define the minimum column and
        attachment boundary.

     X1 COMMENT RETENTION. Use the same retained-ownership transition in
        list items, definition bodies and footnote bodies. A line comment or
        matched comment fence consumed BELOW the content column sets
        retention only when a continuation claim or earlier retention admits
        it. A blank or a comment at or above that column clears retention.
        The admitted comment closes the paragraph through T and stores no
        claim.
        A noninterrupting follower below the column uses retained ownership
        to stay in that container and START a paragraph (CARVE-P9-053).
        Its ordinary-line transition then supplies the new paragraph's claim;
        retaining the owner was not itself a lazy fold. Its limits:
          - A sibling marker is recognized first. A retained list marker
            below the content column remains paragraph text, and closing the
            preceding paragraph does not reduce the indentation a child list
            needs.
          - Without a claim or earlier retention, O2 selects the enclosing
            owner and O4 closes the missed container.
          - Retention describes the most recent boundary, not a later
            visible line across arbitrary invisible boundaries. In
            `- inner` / `  %% c` / ` code`, the at-column comment clears
            retention, so ` code` leaves the item.
          - An interrupting opener below the content column is classified
            outside the container.
          - An unmatched `%%%` fence is a line comment and follows this rule.
            A matched comment fence at the enclosing context's opener column
            ends the item (corpus 214).

     X2 THE QUOTE BAND. Under a quote holding an open paragraph, a line that
        writes no `>` folds into that paragraph in two places where the host
        would otherwise read it: a list marker at every column, and a block
        opener at every column but the host's content column.

        A column cannot supply a quote prefix (O1), but a paragraph open in
        the quote keeps the quote open, and O3 folds into it before the host
        reads the residue. An unmarked list marker under that paragraph
        therefore folds under PART 9 §10 I2, even at an enclosing item's content
        column, where O3 would otherwise hand it to PART 9 §24 C3:

            - a
              > - x
              - m

        This also holds for a quote on the item marker line and at every host
        or quote depth. Quoted prose in place of the quoted list folds alike.
        With an item, heading, fence or paragraph instead of the quote, the
        enclosing item owns the line and PART 9 §24 C3 opens a list element at its
        content column. A blank ends the quote and its nested columns
        (CARVE-P0-019). Intervening lazy prose keeps the quote open, including
        for following markers (CARVE-P0-015). Corpus 448 pins folds and controls.

        A BLOCK OPENER IN THE BAND FOLDS ON THE SAME TERMS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-021].
        Under a quote holding an open paragraph, a code/raw fence, heading,
        thematic break or table row opens a block only at the host's content
        column. At every other column it folds into the paragraph. For
        `- a` / `  > q`, column 2 opens; column 1 and columns 3 through 8 fold,
        including the quote's column 4. Corpus 531 pins this band in all hosts.

        A blank closes the paragraph. In the host, the same opener then opens
        a sibling block on its authored base (carve#2598).

        At document level an indented fence is text at every nonzero column,
        with or without a blank; no container minimum permits rebasing there.
        Apply the host rule equally to list items, definition bodies and
        footnote bodies. In `:: t` / `:  - a` / `    [r]: /url`, the body's
        content column is 3 and the item's is 5; the definition at column 4
        registers in the body (CARVE-P0-020). A newly marked quote after a
        blank does not revive an ended item (CARVE-P0-019).

     X3 THE OPAQUE MARKER-LINE QUOTE.

        AN OPAQUE MARKER-LINE QUOTE PRESERVES THE BAND AS TEXT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-022].
        When a code or raw fence opens inside the quote prefix on a list item's
        marker line, an over-indented follower establishes no fresh authored
        base while that fence is open or remains the quote's last block. A later
        open code or raw fence in that same quote also preserves the band;
        closing that later fence restores the authored-base behavior of
        CARVE-P0-004. This applies
        at any quote depth. A quote marker in the band remains literal item
        text; it supplies no prefix to the preceding quote. A line at the item's
        content column still opens there. A blank permits a new authored base.
        A later quoted paragraph ends the fence-tail condition. A heading,
        table or thematic break quote head retains authored-base behavior.
        Comment fences remain governed by PART 9 §28.

     CONSEQUENCES. Each clause below is decided by the step it names.

        THE MARKER LINE'S CONTENT IS THE ITEM'S FIRST BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-007].
        Marker-line content is the item's first block and answers O3's test
        like any other block. Headings, tables, thematic breaks, comments,
        link/footnote definitions and floating attributes leave no paragraph
        open, so a following line below the content column closes the item,
        the band above its base included. Ordinary prose, or a container
        ending in an open paragraph, permits a lazy fold.

        A terminated comment span on a nested item's marker line is that
        item's first block. In `- a` / `  - %%%` / `    hidden` / `%%%` /
        `tail`, `tail` is a document paragraph at every closer column. An
        ancestor MUST NOT treat the closer as a fresh below-column comment
        granting retention. An ordinary payload line below the inner item's
        content column still ends that item under O1 and O4. Definitions are
        collected where written (CARVE-P9-034); item closure governs the next line
        and MUST NOT discard the definition.

        A FLOATING ATTRIBUTE BLOCK DOES NOT CHANGE THE CONTENT COLUMN --
        NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-008]. Marker-attached attributes contribute zero
        width to `content_column` (PART 9 §24 C3). A task checkbox is content, not
        marker width. Thus `-{#item} {#next}` / `  # H` keeps the item open and
        attaches `{#next}` to the heading at column 2. An ordered `1.` marker
        places the equivalent heading at column 3.

        Moving the heading one column left closes the item: the floating
        attribute opened no paragraph. Reclassify the residue as document
        paragraph text and discard the item's pending attribute (CARVE-P9-024).
        It MUST NOT escape to that paragraph. A task item retains its checkbox
        even when no block remains. Ordinary marker-line prose, which opens a
        paragraph, permits a below-column lazy fold.

        A sibling marker also closes the item and discards pending attributes.
        In `- a` / `  -{#p} b` / `    {#x}` / `  - c`, `{#x}` belongs to the
        first sub-item and is dropped when `- c` closes it. The sublist remains
        one list. For quote content, inspect the last block recursively:
        `- > q` / `tail` folds; `- > # H` / `tail` does not.

        A DEFINITION AT THE CONTENT COLUMN ENDS THE PARAGRAPH, NOT THE
        CONTAINER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-010]. A link or footnote definition
        at a list item's or definition body's `content_column` interrupts the
        paragraph and registers (PART 9 §10 I5). A comment there also interrupts.
        The container stays open until a later line is owned elsewhere (O2).
        Thus `- a` / `  [r]: /u` / `tail` registers `r`, then ends the item and
        publishes `tail` at document level. A definition registers only in
        the owner O4 reads it in: one below a body's content column does not
        register in that body.

        At document column 0 a definition is a document-level interrupter.
        At a nonzero column below `content_column` it is lazy paragraph text
        and does not register: `- a` / ` [r]: /u` / `tail` folds. Abbreviation
        definitions are recognized only at document level (CARVE-P12-026); inside a
        container their lines are ordinary text and reopen the paragraph.

        A QUOTE IS REACHED BY ITS MARKER, AND A COLUMN NEVER REACHES INTO ONE.
        Prefix matching requires `>` (O1); indentation cannot supply it.
        An unmarked definition line reaching it through lazy continuation is
        paragraph text and registers nothing. With the marker supplied,
        `> - x` / `>   [r]: /u` registers at the inner item's content column.

        A NEW MARKER DOES NOT REACH A DEAD CONTAINER'S COLUMN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-019].
        A blank ends a block quote and the containers opened inside it. A later
        quote marker opens a new quote without inheriting their columns.

            > - x

            >   [r]: /u

            > [r][]

        No item is open when the definition is read, so its extra indentation
        makes it paragraph text and `[r][]` remains literal. Without the blank
        after `> - x`, the definition registers in the open item.

        With no item open, definitions register at the context's content
        column exactly, or at document column 0. Extra indentation provides
        no registration site. With an item open, PART 9 §24 C3 accepts at or past its
        content column by erasing the authored block base.

        AT OR PAST MEANS THE DEEPEST COLUMN THE LINE REACHES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-020].
        Register a definition in the innermost open container whose content
        column it reaches. A deeper container that the line does not reach
        neither owns nor withholds it. In `- - x` / `   [r]: /url`, the items'
        content columns are 2 and 4; the definition at column 3 registers in
        the outer item. Columns 2, 4 and 5 also register. Past a content column,
        extra indentation may change the owner but never remove registration.
        Corpus 441 pins the four columns. A block standing open does not
        withhold the columns below its own either: a definition it does not
        itself register ends its extent above that line, and the line takes
        its base in the container whose content column it does reach.

        BELOW EVERY OPEN COLUMN IS UNCHANGED. A nonzero-column definition
        below every open content column remains lazy paragraph text and does
        not register (PART 9 §24 C3).

        AT A CONTAINER'S CONTENT COLUMN, A BLOCK ENDS THE PARAGRAPH IT SITS
        UNDER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-011]. A block at the content column is
        recognized as at document column 0 (PART 9 §24 C3). It ends the preceding
        paragraph and leaves the container open, whether the block is visible
        or invisible.
        Comments, link/footnote definitions and attribute blocks all apply.
        A following document-column line leaves the container if no open
        paragraph remains anywhere in the stack.

        This transition covers the block's entire extent (CARVE-P0-012).

        A HEADING IS THE VISIBLE CONTROL -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-012]. A heading
        at the content column leaves no paragraph open. A following line at
        document column 0 ends the item under O4, as after a marker-line
        heading or a heading inside a quote. A line at the item's content column
        remains its second paragraph. A line at a nonzero column below it
        stays only by O3 or X1.

        DEFINITION EXTENT. A footnote definition's extent is its marker line,
        the continuation lines reaching its body column (two columns past the
        column the definition is written at) and the blanks between its
        blocks; no lazy line continues into it. `- a` / `  [^f]: t` / blank /
        `    more` / `tail` ends the item and gives the note two blocks. A link
        reference definition is one line: a blank after it is outside the
        definition, following indented prose opens the container's second
        paragraph, and a flush-left line folds into it. Nested definition
        extent is not decided by this clause.

        A FENCED BODY IS NOT A PARAGRAPH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-013]. If a line
        supplies no indentation prefix to the fence's host, O1 stops there and
        its opaque-body rule does not apply. A verbatim body holds no open
        paragraph for O3 to continue. In

            - ```
            x
            ```

        the item closes with an empty code block; `x` is document text and the
        trailing run is ordinary inline text.

        Where no container in the stack holds an open paragraph, select the
        nearest surviving ancestor whose content column the line reaches and
        close intervening containers at every depth. The inner item keeps its
        fence body; reclassify the run in the selected owner at its own column
        (carve#2490). With no open paragraph at document level, a run at
        document column 0 opens a code block. With inner
        content column 4, a run at column 0 or 1 reaches the document; a run at
        column 2 reaches an outer item.

        NO HOST TERM. This holds for items, definition bodies
        and footnote bodies inside quotes too: an unmarked column-0 line
        reaches the document. Matched `%%%` spans are the exception (PART 9 §28):
        their whitespace slot admits a closer at any column, ending the span
        without ending a container.

        FENCE KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-014].
        Container reach depends on whether an open paragraph exists in the
        stack. Code bodies cannot hold one; colon bodies can. A column-0 line
        ends the host when no paragraph is open; with one open, it folds (O3).

        A code fence interrupts an open paragraph only when a closer exists
        (PART 9 §10 I4). Otherwise its line is paragraph text, including at a
        container's content column.

        THE CLOSER LOOKAHEAD DOES NOT STOP AT A BELOW-COLUMN LINE. Search past
        such lines when deciding whether the fence has a closer.
        A BELOW-COLUMN LINE IS SEARCHED PAST, NEVER MATCHED. Count only closers
        inside the container. In

            - a
              ```
              b
             y
              ```

        the indented closer makes a real code block, which ends the item at
        ` y`. Moving the closer to document column 0 makes it ineligible;
        no block opens, and ` y` and the run fold as paragraph text. A blank
        closes the paragraph; a terminated fence leaves no paragraph open.

        A REAL DIV IS A CONTAINER LIKE ANY OTHER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-016].
        An unterminated colon div permits a lazy fold when its last block is
        an open paragraph: `- item` / `  ::: note` / `  body` / `tail` folds.
        Omitting `body` leaves an empty div and no paragraph to continue, so
        `tail` is top-level. Closing the div also closes its paragraph and
        leaves `tail` at top level.

        CONTAINER KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-017].
        Apply the same test in quotes. `> quote` / `> ::: note` / `tail` ends
        the quote, as does the form with `> body` / `> :::` before `tail`.
        An empty or closed colon container leaves no open paragraph.

        DEFINITION BODIES FOLLOW THE SAME CONTAINER REACH RULE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-018].
        A definition body's canonical prefix reaches column 2. In

            :: t
            : ```
            body
            ```

        `body` misses that prefix. O1 stops at the definition entry; the
        opaque-body rule does not apply and O3 finds no open paragraph. The
        body closes with an empty code block, and `body` and the trailing run
        are reclassified at document level, as for the list-item form
        (CARVE-P0-013).

        The hosts agree on an unfinished fence on a NESTED marker line too. In

            :: t
            : - ``` x
            code

        `code` misses every prefix: the nested item closes with an empty code
        block and `code` is document text, as it is under a `- - ` marker line
        (corpus 455).
   OUTPUT. The layout-resolved line stream (equivalently: INDENT / DEDENT /
           CONT events plus line residues). PARTS 1-2 parse blocks over it;
           inline parsing (PART 3) runs per finished block -- the two-phase
           model of PART 8.
   ============================================================================ *)
